#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mesh-card — a node's small, current-state self-description (durable memory tier).
#
# Unlike the trace (volatile history), the card is the node's *now*: capabilities,
# known peers, and — the part that matters most — the INVARIANT this node must hold.
#
# The card is self-truthing: `--refresh` regenerates the live block from actual
# state (routing, exit-node, wireguard, services) and CHECKS it against the
# invariant. The VPN regression of 2026-06-07 happened because the card froze as
# a hand-written intention and silently diverged from reality; --refresh exists so
# that can't happen quietly again.
#
#   mesh-card             # print the card
#   mesh-card --refresh   # regenerate the live block from real state; check invariant
#   mesh-card --roster    # the sticky organ roster (ever-declared organs + retired ones)
#   mesh-card --retire-organ <organ>   # the only way an organ leaves the card (see STICKY ROSTER)
#
# Hand-written sections (invariant:, notes:) are preserved across --refresh.
# The live block is auto-generated — do not hand-edit it.
#
# A systemd watchdog (mesh-card-watchdog.timer + .service) runs --refresh
# every 15 minutes and alerts via mesh-tg on violation. Enable:
#   systemctl --user enable --now mesh-card-watchdog.timer
#
# ── CODE DUALITY (Hoffmeyer & Emmeche; Pattee) ──────────────────────────────────
# LITERATURE — biosemiotics, an OPERATIONAL mechanism (2026-06-21): CODE DUALITY (Claus Emmeche
# & Jesper Hoffmeyer, "From language to nature — the semiotic metamorphosis of biology", Semiotica
# 84:1, 1991; written 1988; roots in Bateson's analog/digital codes + Howard Pattee's complementarity
# of time-INDEPENDENT DESCRIPTION vs time-DEPENDENT DYNAMICS). Life requires TWO interlocking codes:
# an ANALOG code for ACTION/behaviour (the embodied, dynamic, time-dependent organism — the string
# quartet's actual performance) and a DIGITAL code for MEMORY/inheritance (a discrete, re-describable,
# time-independent record — the score). A living system PERSISTS across reproduction only because the
# analog organism is periodically RE-DESCRIBED into the digital code, and the digital code is later
# RE-EXPRESSED to reconstruct a new analog. Two faithfulness requirements fall out: (1) the digital
# re-description must not DIVERGE from the analog it describes; (2) it must be COMPLETE enough that
# re-expression reconstructs the FUNCTIONAL analog, not a crippled ancestor.
# THE MESH IS LITERALLY CODE-DUAL: the GENOME (scripts/, git — digital, re-describable, inheritable,
# time-independent) is the score; the RUNNING mesh (deployed ~/.local/bin executing + live panes/
# processes/senses — analog, dynamic, time-dependent) is the performance. THIS CARD is the per-node
# DIGITAL re-description: `--refresh` re-describes live analog state INTO the card (analog→digital),
# and on reboot `mesh-restore` TRUSTS the card to reconstruct the analog session (digital→analog). The
# reboot doctrine ("clean reincarnation: same hostname + ~/.mesh-card, fresh session") IS Emmeche-
# Hoffmeyer reproduction: the analog dies, the digital seed rebuilds it.
# WHERE THE CARD ALREADY EMBODIES IT — and where it STOPS: --refresh fights requirement (1) for the
# SUBSTRATE/CAPABILITY analog (the VPN-2026-06-07 divergence is a pure code-duality fault — the digital
# froze as intention and drifted from the analog reality; the :66 under-declare + :104 over-declare
# phantom-capability hazards are the same divergence in either direction). But it under-serves
# requirement (2): the card re-describes the STATIC substrate/capability snapshot, NOT the LEARNED /
# ADAPTIVE analog state a running node accrues — an empirically-tuned threshold a mind found works, an
# accreted baseline, an organ verified-working-once. That state is a THIRD category between the two the
# mesh handles cleanly: it is NOT re-observable perception (rightly DECAYED on reboot — "perception is
# re-observed, never stored") and NOT genome code (rightly in git). Code duality names exactly why this
# middle band silently fails to survive reproduction: it lives ONLY in the analog code, no digital
# re-description captures it, so each reincarnation inherits the genome's ANCESTRAL DEFAULTS — the
# functional adaptations the running node learned do not inherit. (Complement to this session's
# evolutionary-activity review on mesh-vitality: that asks whether produced structure PERSISTS; this
# asks whether learned structure is RE-DESCRIBED so it can persist ACROSS a reboot at all.)
# HELD FIX (instrument-first, steward/operator-gated — behavioral): a narrow, opt-in analog→digital
# re-description channel in the card for the LEARNED-adaptation band only — a tool declares a small,
# named, durable parameter (with provenance) that --refresh captures and mesh-restore re-expresses, so
# a reincarnated node inherits the adaptation, not the ancestral default. WHY HELD: the hard part is
# the BOUNDARY — most analog state is intentionally-decayed perception, and over-capturing would smuggle
# transient noise into the durable tier (re-introducing the very divergence --refresh exists to kill,
# and bloating the reproduction seed). Which parameters are worth re-describing must be curated +
# validated (against reboot-survival history) before any capture is wired. NOT shipped.
# DISTINCT from prior biosemiotics reviews: NOT the INTERPRETANT (triadic sign — the third leg of
# meaning); NOT Deacon SYMBOL-GROUNDING (icon→index→symbol reference stability). Code duality is about
# the DUAL-CODE ARCHITECTURE (analog action ⇄ digital memory) and the re-description that carries a
# system across reproduction — the inheritance mechanism, not the sign-reference relation.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
set -uo pipefail
# mesh-card calls many sibling tools (mesh-power, mesh-vitals, mesh-isp-health, mesh-peers) by name.
# Over a raw ssh/non-interactive shell ~/.local/bin may be absent from PATH, silently degrading every
# one of them (e.g. known-peers fell back to the non-deduped scan on ds). Anchor PATH so the card
# behaves identically wherever it runs.
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
# SELF — an ABSOLUTE path to THIS file, resolved before any test arm rewrites HOME. The --test arms
# re-invoke the card as a child (`"$SELF" --refresh`); a bare "$0" has no slash when the tool is run
# off PATH, so bash would resolve it through PATH — which :76 has just anchored to the ORIGINAL
# $HOME/.local/bin — and the child would be the DEPLOYED copy, not the file under test. A repo run of
# `bash mesh-card --test` then greens off code that is not in this file
# ([[a-hand-run-repo-copy-masks-the-deployed-child]], inverted).
SELF="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)/$(basename "$0")"

CARD="${MESH_CARD:-$HOME/.mesh-card}"

# Default invariant, seeded only if the card has none yet. Edit it in the card.
DEFAULT_INVARIANT="this node's control plane (SSH/Tailscale/own egress) stays on the clean
  default route, always reachable. A capability this node OFFERS (e.g. VPN egress)
  must never carry this node's own reachability. Only forwarded client traffic may
  ride an offered route. Blast radius matches the request; nothing global, nothing
  involuntary."

ts() { date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ; }

# --- preserve hand-written sections from an existing card ---------------------
extract_invariant() {
    [ -r "$CARD" ] || { printf '%s\n' "  $DEFAULT_INVARIANT"; return; }
    local inv
    inv=$(awk '
        /^invariant:/ {grab=1; next}
        grab && /^[a-z].*:/ {grab=0}
        grab {print}
    ' "$CARD" | sed '/^[[:space:]]*$/d')
    if [ -n "$inv" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$inv"
    else
        printf '%s\n' "  $DEFAULT_INVARIANT"
    fi
}
extract_notes() {
    [ -r "$CARD" ] || return
    awk '/^notes:/{grab=1} grab{print}' "$CARD"
}

# --- live probes (all sudo-free) ----------------------------------------------
# minds_off_by_channels [restore-env-path] → rc0 if a lean-node MESH_MIND_CHANNELS allowlist
# (restore.env) is set but overlaps NONE of the real mind-bearing channels — i.e. no mind engine
# will ever be launched here regardless of installed binaries. Mirrors the SAME lean-allowlist
# gate mesh-restore/-channel-keepalive/-mind-keepalive already apply before launching an engine
# into a channel (scripts/mesh-restore:353, -channel-keepalive:485, -mind-keepalive:98) — but
# applied here at the CARD (the source every one of those tools trusts for `minds:`) instead of
# only at each individual launch site. Channel list mirrors mesh-restore's own uniform 13-channel
# set (scripts/mesh-restore ~L424) so it stays in sync by inspection.
# CODE-CONFIRMED root cause (chat-review 2026-07-08T09:49:11Z, owner: mesh-card/genome): ds/
# Default-string's restore.env carries MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="none" (deliberate off-switch — "none"
# matches no real channel name) yet its card kept auto-declaring 'minds: claude opencode ollama'
# from pure binary detection, so mesh-restore/dispatch/channel-keepalive — which all trust the
# card, not restore.env directly — kept treating a policy-OFF node as a live mind node.
# Path arg is test-only (avoids clobbering the live ~/.mesh/restore.env during --test).
minds_off_by_channels() {
    local env_file="${1:-$HOME/.mesh/restore.env}"
    local MESH_MIND_CHANNELS=""
    [ -r "$env_file" ] && . "$env_file" 2>/dev/null
    [ -n "${MESH_MIND_CHANNELS:-}" ] || return 1   # unset = full uniform suite, not off
    local w
    for w in minds genome tg senses health chat room pub discover sound vpn bruno witness; do
        case " $MESH_MIND_CHANNELS " in *" $w "*) return 1 ;; esac
    done
    return 0   # allowlist present but overlaps NO real channel -> effectively off
}

# --- S5 IDENTITY COHERENCE — VSM pathology PII2, "institutional schizophrenia" ----------------
# LITERATURE (live review 2026-08-04): José Pérez Ríos, "The Viable System Model and the Taxonomy
# of Organizational Pathologies in the Age of Artificial Intelligence (AI)", *Systems* 13(9):749
# (29 Aug 2025), doi:10.3390/systems13090749 — the current restatement of his Taxonomy of
# Organizational Pathologies (TOP), read in full for this review; the same taxonomy in Pérez Ríos,
# "Models of organizational cybernetics for diagnosis and design", *Kybernetes* 39(9/10):1529-1550
# (2010), doi:10.1108/03684921011081222 (26 pathologies, groups I/II/III), also read.
#
#   PII2. Institutional schizophrenia. "Two or more different identity conceptions produce
#   conflict within an organization."  (Pérez Ríos 2025, §2.3.2, Pathologies Related to System 5)
#
# Beer's System 5 IS identity: who this node is and what it is for. On a mesh node that identity is
# not held in one place — it is declared across `~/.mesh-card` (`minds:`), `~/.mesh/restore.env`
# (per-channel `MESH_*_CMD`, `MESH_MIND_CHANNELS`, `MESH_RETIRED_CHANNELS`), and the doctrine that
# the CARD is authoritative. When two of those surfaces carry DIFFERENT conceptions of the same
# thing, the node holds two identities at once and each tool acts on whichever surface it reads.
#
# The mesh has already paid for exactly this shape: `minds_off_by_channels` above exists ONLY
# because ds's card kept declaring `minds: claude opencode ollama` while its restore.env said
# `MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="none"` — two conceptions, and every card-trusting tool believed the wrong
# one. That fix RECONCILES one specific pair at the moment of writing; it does not MEASURE how many
# such conflicts the node is carrying. This does.
#
# What counts as a conflict (all report-only — the card never fails on it):
#   1. `redeclared` — the same MESH_* key assigned twice in restore.env with DIFFERENT source text.
#      Shell sourcing is last-wins, so the LATER line is what runs; the earlier one is a dead
#      conception that still READS as live to a human or an agent grepping the file. Same shape as
#      "a stale Wants= resurrects a retired organ" and "a constant outlives its reader".
#      Identical re-assignment is redundancy, NOT schizophrenia — it is not flagged.
#   2. `planted+retired` — a channel named in BOTH MESH_MIND_CHANNELS and MESH_RETIRED_CHANNELS:
#      the node declares the same channel alive and decommissioned simultaneously.
#   3. `card-vs-policy` — the card declares `minds:` non-empty while the restore.env allowlist says
#      no mind channel exists here (the ds regression, measured instead of silently reconciled).
#
# Comparison is on SOURCE TEXT, deliberately: `A="$X"` and `A="$Y"` may expand to the same string,
# but PII2 is about the conceptions a reader holds, and a reader reads the text.
#
# Path args are test-only (so --test never reads or writes the live node's surfaces).
identity_conflicts() {
    local env_file="${1:-$HOME/.mesh/restore.env}" card_file="${2:-$CARD}"
    # n/a must be a CLAIM about the node, never a silent COHERENT: no restore.env = cannot ask.
    [ -r "$env_file" ] || return 2

    # (1) same key, two different source values. mawk-safe (no gawk 3-arg match()); a value that
    # opens with a quote is taken to its CLOSING quote, so a '#' inside the value is not mistaken
    # for a trailing comment. A commented-out declaration is not a declaration — the anchor rejects
    # any leading '#', which is what keeps restore.env's several `# MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="..."`
    # archaeology lines out of the count.
    awk '
      /^[ \t]*(export[ \t]+)?MESH_[A-Za-z0-9_]+=/ {
        line = $0
        sub(/^[ \t]*(export[ \t]+)?/, "", line)
        eq = index(line, "=")
        k  = substr(line, 1, eq - 1)
        v  = substr(line, eq + 1)
        q  = substr(v, 1, 1)
        if (q == "\"" || q == "'\''") {
          rest = substr(v, 2); c = index(rest, q)
          v = (c > 0) ? substr(rest, 1, c - 1) : rest
        } else {
          sub(/[ \t;#].*$/, "", v)
        }
        if ((k in seen) && seen[k] != v)
          printf "redeclared %s: L%d=\"%s\" -> L%d=\"%s\" (earlier line is dead but reads as live)\n", \
                 k, ln[k], seen[k], NR, v
        seen[k] = v; ln[k] = NR
      }
    ' "$env_file"

    # (2)+(3) need the EFFECTIVE (last-wins) values, so source rather than re-parse — but in a
    # SUBSHELL: restore.env exports dozens of vars and sourcing it into the card's own process
    # would let the file under inspection rewrite the inspector's environment.
    local eff planted retired ch
    # shellcheck disable=SC1090
    eff=$( . "$env_file" 2>/dev/null; printf '%s\n%s\n' "${MESH_MIND_CHANNELS:-}" "${MESH_RETIRED_CHANNELS:-}" )
    planted=$(printf '%s\n' "$eff" | sed -n 1p)
    retired=$(printf '%s\n' "$eff" | sed -n 2p)
    for ch in $planted; do
        case " $retired " in
            *" $ch "*) echo "planted+retired $ch: named in BOTH MESH_MIND_CHANNELS and MESH_RETIRED_CHANNELS" ;;
        esac
    done
    local card_minds=""
    [ -r "$card_file" ] && card_minds=$(sed -n 's/^  minds: //p' "$card_file" | head -1)
    case "$card_minds" in ""|"(none)") ;; *)
        minds_off_by_channels "$env_file" \
          && echo "card-vs-policy: card declares 'minds: $card_minds' but MESH_MIND_CHANNELS names no real channel (node is policy-OFF)" ;;
    esac
    return 0
}

# One card line from the conflict list. Never invents a verdict it could not compute.
identity_verdict() {
    local out rc n
    out=$(identity_conflicts "$@"); rc=$?
    [ "$rc" = 2 ] && { echo "n/a (no readable restore.env — cannot ask)"; return; }
    n=$(printf '%s' "$out" | grep -c . )
    [ "$n" = 0 ] && { echo "COHERENT"; return; }
    printf '⚠ CONFLICTED (%s — PII2 institutional schizophrenia; see `mesh-card --identity`): %s\n' \
        "$n" "$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | head -1 | cut -c1-110)"
}

detect_minds() {
    [ "${MESH_NO_CAP_MINDS:-0}" = 1 ] && { echo ""; return; }
    minds_off_by_channels && { echo ""; return; }
    local m=""
    # Probe under the node's REAL toolchain, not a bare non-login PATH: minds install in many places
    # (nvm node bin, ~/.opencode/bin, ~/.bun/bin, ~/.local/bin). A bare command-v misses them → the
    # card under-declares → mesh-restore (which trusts the card) rebuilds FEWER mind channels than the
    # node actually runs (2026-06-13: IdeaPad lost opencode[~/.opencode/bin]+codex[nvm] on restart).
    [ -s "$HOME/.nvm/nvm.sh" ] && . "$HOME/.nvm/nvm.sh" >/dev/null 2>&1 && nvm use node >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    local d; for d in "$HOME/.opencode/bin" "$HOME/.bun/bin" "$HOME/.local/bin"; do
        [ -d "$d" ] && case ":$PATH:" in *":$d:"*) :;; *) PATH="$d:$PATH";; esac
    done
    for a in claude opencode gemini codex aider ollama llm; do
        command -v "$a" >/dev/null 2>&1 && m="$m $a"
    done
    echo "${m# }"
}
# mic is REAL only if it CAPTURES a frame — not merely listed by `arecord -l`. A headless VM lists a
# dummy card that never yields audio (hollow organ → cascades into mesh-transcribe/-organ/-tts FAILs,
# 2026-06-12 on ds). Mirrors mesh-doctor's busy-vs-broken probe and shares its lock so card and doctor
# don't fight over the device. busy (held by a voice agent) = real; no-frame-and-not-busy = hollow.
# Probes EVERY capture device arecord lists, not just plughw:0,0 — the working mic may be a USB
# card at index 1+ while card 0 is a dummy (ds: card hid a verified-working USB mic, 2026-06-12).
mic_real() {
    local devs wav="/tmp/.card-mic.$$.wav" err="/tmp/.card-mic.$$.err" dev rc=1
    if command -v arecord >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        # BOTH reads bounded (2026-08-24): a wedged sound card hangs `arecord -l` outright, and a
        # device that goes catatonic AFTER the lock is taken hangs the capture with the lock held —
        # the -w 8 bounds only the WAIT for the lock, never the arecord that follows it.
        devs=$(_probe_out mic arecord -l | sed -n 's/^card \([0-9]\+\): .*device \([0-9]\+\):.*/\1,\2/p')
        for dev in $devs; do
            timeout -k 2 "$PROBE_SECS" flock -w 8 /tmp/.mesh-mic.lock arecord -d 1 -D "plughw:$dev" -f S16_LE -r 16000 "$wav" >/dev/null 2>"$err"
            case $? in 124|137) _probe_mark_unknown mic ;; esac
            if [ -s "$wav" ] || grep -qiE 'busy|in use|resource busy' "$err" 2>/dev/null; then rc=0; break; fi
        done
    fi
    # PipeWire fallback: on a desktop session where PipeWire/PulseAudio owns the capture device
    # EXCLUSIVELY, raw ALSA hw access reads busy/silent and the loop above leaves rc=1 — yet a real
    # mic is right there THROUGH PipeWire. ds 2026-06-26 (discover↻+health BATON): the webcam mic
    # pcmC2D0c is held by pipewire → `arecord -D hw:2` empty/no-busy → mic UNDECLARED, though
    # `pw-record` captures a ~125KB live WAV. Mirror the mesh-camera ffmpeg→fswebcam gate: detect a
    # sense by the SAME path the live organ (mesh-hear ds backend) takes, never a subset that buries
    # a working ear. Non-empty capture = device produced frames = real (same any-frame rule as above).
    if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] && command -v pw-record >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}" \
            timeout 3 pw-record --rate 16000 --channels 1 --format s16 "$wav" >/dev/null 2>"$err"
        [ -s "$wav" ] && rc=0
    fi
    rm -f "$wav" "$err"; return "$rc"
}

# ── DECLARATION vs ATTAINMENT (organ-roster-shrink-hides-a-dead-organ, 2026-08-17) ─────────────────
# A card DECLARES what this node IS (configuration — a durable, intentional fact); mesh-organ-keepalive
# reports what it currently ATTAINS (reachability — a live, transient fact). Fusing the two is the bug
# this block exists to prevent: detect_senses used to gate a phone-body sense on the organ's OWN live
# probe (`mesh-body-motion --test`, which SSHes the phone and exits 2 when it is unreachable). So the
# moment the body dropped, the sense left the CARD — and keepalive only probes DECLARED organs, so the
# one failure class the DARK path exists to catch (total organ loss) was the one it could never see.
# LIVE and TOTAL shrank together and the count read 12/12, 0 DARK, while the organ was gone. Measured
# 2026-08-17 (health@, board organ-roster-shrink-hides-a-dead-organ): FOUR roster transitions in 41
# minutes, 0 DARK across all of them; the card at 19:53Z declared `ble mic camera irq light link-flap`
# — body-motion simply absent, no alarm anywhere.
# RULE: declare on CONFIGURATION (the tool is installed AND this node has the thing configured), never
# on a live network probe. The reachability verdict belongs entirely to mesh-organ-keepalive, which
# has the 2-consecutive debounce for it (both edges) and the board escalation.
BODY_CFG_FILE="${MESH_NODES_FILE:-$HOME/.mesh/nodes}"
# body_configured — does this node have a phone BODY configured? Reads the node registry as TEXT (a
# configuration fact) rather than sourcing it: sourcing would import every export in ~/.mesh/nodes into
# a card refresh. An already-exported PHONE_* address counts too (a caller that set one means it).
body_configured() {
    [ -n "${PHONE_IP:-}${PHONE_LAN_IPS:-}${PHONE_TS_IP:-}" ] && return 0
    [ -r "$BODY_CFG_FILE" ] || return 1
    grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*(export[[:space:]]+)?PHONE_(IP|LAN_IPS|TS_IP)=[^[:space:]#]' "$BODY_CFG_FILE"
}
# _has_webcam / _has_physical_link — the other two configuration facts, both local hardware, no probe.
_has_webcam() { ls /dev/video0 >/dev/null 2>&1; }
_has_physical_link() {  # a real NIC: carrier_changes + (a /device link OR a /wireless dir), like
    local i                              # mesh-link-flap's own select_links — tunnels do not count.
    for i in "${MESH_NET_DIR:-/sys/class/net}"/*; do
        [ -r "$i/carrier_changes" ] || continue
        [ "$(basename "$i")" = lo ] && continue
        { [ -e "$i/device" ] || [ -d "$i/wireless" ]; } && return 0
    done
    return 1
}

# ── STICKY ROSTER: once declared, an organ can only leave by an explicit retire ────────────────────
# The declaration gate above stops the phone-body case, but ANY detector can go momentarily false
# (a webcam mid-reset, bluetoothctl mid-restart) and take its organ out of the denominator with it.
# The roster is the monotone floor: DECLARED = detected ∪ ever-declared − retired. A probe failure
# can then only move an organ from LIVE to DARK; it can never shrink TOTAL. Removal is deliberate:
#   mesh-card --retire-organ <name>    (and --roster to see the roster + what is retired)
ORGAN_ROSTER="${MESH_ORGAN_ROSTER:-$HOME/.mesh/organ-roster}"     # "<class> <organ> <first-declared>"
ORGAN_RETIRED="${MESH_ORGAN_RETIRED:-$HOME/.mesh/organ-retired}"  # one organ name per line
_organ_retired() { [ -r "$ORGAN_RETIRED" ] && grep -qxF "$1" "$ORGAN_RETIRED"; }
# roster_declared <class> <detected…> → the declared set for that class (union, retired removed),
# admitting anything newly detected into the roster on the way through.
roster_declared() {
    local class="$1"; shift
    local detected="$*" o known out=""
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$ORGAN_ROSTER")" 2>/dev/null || true
    known="$(awk -v c="$class" '$1==c{print $2}' "$ORGAN_ROSTER" 2>/dev/null)"
    for o in $detected; do
        _organ_retired "$o" && continue
        printf '%s\n' "$known" | grep -qxF "$o" && continue
        printf '%s %s %s\n' "$class" "$o" "$(date -u +%FT%TZ)" >> "$ORGAN_ROSTER" 2>/dev/null || true
        known="$known
$o"
    done
    for o in $(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$(printf '%s' "$detected" | tr ' ' '\n')" "$known" \
                 | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | sort -u); do
        _organ_retired "$o" || out="$out $o"
    done
    printf '%s' "${out# }"
}

detect_senses() {
    local s=""
    # COUPLING-CONSTITUTION FALLACY (Adams & Aizawa; the canonical 4E/extended-cognition critique,
    # live 2023-25 "Mark of the Cognitive" debate, PMC5712358): a resource being causally COUPLED to a
    # cognitive system does NOT make it a CONSTITUENT of it. This detector declares a SENSE present by
    # COUPLING — the tool is installed (`command -v X`, :100) or passes a smoke `--test` (:106/111/112)
    # — NOT by CONSTITUTION (it produced a genuine recent reading). Installed / smoke-green != produces
    # real perception. PROOF it self-deceives: mesh-baro was cron-wired AND smoke-GREEN yet a DARK sense
    # (the only embodied node has no barometer → 0 real reads in ~300 runs; honest-decayed 2026-06-21) —
    # a phantom capability in the card, which claims to be "self-truthing" (:7) but here over-declares
    # the self's sensory boundary. Same failure CLASS the mesh keeps re-patching ad hoc and the 4E
    # critique NAMES once: cached Tailscale Online != alive ([[liveness-is-lease-not-cached-status]]),
    # a live mind PROCESS != a working mind ([[blocked-mind-is-a-failure-class]]), a sensor TOKEN != a
    # true reading (the 2026-06-21 mesh-correlate stuck-sensor finding). Coupling is not constitution.
    # THE HELD FIX THAT USED TO SIT HERE IS WITHDRAWN — DO NOT ADOPT IT (2026-08-17). It proposed
    # gating a sense on "evidence of a genuine recent reading" (a fresh non-UNKNOWN ~/.mesh/.<sense>-state
    # artifact, or a contribution-probing --test). That ties DECLARATION even tighter to FRESHNESS and
    # deepens exactly the hole above: a sense whose body has gone away has no fresh state artifact, so
    # the stricter gate would drop it from the card FASTER and more silently, and keepalive — which
    # probes only declared organs — would go blind to it sooner. The over-declare failure it was aimed
    # at (mesh-baro: cron-wired, smoke-green, zero real reads) is real, but its remedy is a real-read
    # ATTAINMENT probe in mesh-organ-keepalive rendering the organ DARK, not a shrinking declaration.
    # Declaration answers "is this node supposed to have this?"; only attainment answers "does it work?".
    # phone (termux) organs
    for nb in battery:termux-battery-status camera:termux-camera-photo \
              mic:termux-microphone-record location:termux-location; do
        command -v "${nb#*:}" >/dev/null 2>&1 && s="$s ${nb%%:*}"
    done
    # linux organs — declare what's actually present (live-contract: card reflects reality)
    _probe_out ble bluetoothctl show | grep -q 'Powered: yes' && s="$s ble"
    _probe_out wifi nmcli -t radio wifi | grep -q enabled && s="$s wifi"
    mic_real && s="$s mic"
    command -v mesh-camera >/dev/null 2>&1 && _probe camera mesh-camera --test && s="$s camera"
    command -v mesh-irq-rate >/dev/null 2>&1 && s="$s irq"
    ls /dev/ttyUSB* /dev/ttyACM* >/dev/null 2>&1 && { command -v screen >/dev/null 2>&1 || command -v minicom >/dev/null 2>&1 || command -v picocom >/dev/null 2>&1 || command -v cu >/dev/null 2>&1; } && s="$s serial"
    ls /sys/class/power_supply/ 2>/dev/null | grep -q . && s="$s power"
    [ -r /proc/acpi/button/lid/LID0/state ] && command -v mesh-lid >/dev/null 2>&1 && s="$s lid"
    # phone-body organs — declared on CONFIGURATION, never on the organ's own live probe (see the
    # DECLARATION vs ATTAINMENT block above). body-motion needs a body; light degrades to a local
    # webcam/beacon when the body is down, so either configuration declares it. link-flap is NOT a
    # phone organ at all (it reads /sys/class/net on THIS node — the old comment mis-grouped it):
    # its configuration fact is that the node has a physical NIC.
    command -v mesh-body-motion >/dev/null 2>&1 && body_configured && s="$s body-motion"
    command -v mesh-light >/dev/null 2>&1 && { body_configured || _has_webcam; } && s="$s light"
    command -v mesh-link-flap >/dev/null 2>&1 && _has_physical_link && s="$s link-flap"
    # net-vantage: a public-IP vantage — a globally-routable IPv4 bound LOCALLY (not LAN/CGNAT/wg).
    # The sense mesh-watchtower reads (inbound knocks / external reach / self-exposure). Detect the
    # ADDRESS, not the tool: mesh-* tools propagate to every node, so mesh-watchtower presence would
    # make every node falsely claim it — only a node with its own public IP genuinely has the sense.
    ip -4 -o addr show scope global 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $4}' | cut -d/ -f1 \
      | grep -qvE '^(10\.|172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\.|192\.168\.|100\.(6[4-9]|[7-9][0-9]|1[01][0-9]|12[0-7])\.|127\.|169\.254\.|0\.)' \
      && s="$s net-vantage"
    echo "${s# }"
}
_wg_has_active_peers() {
    local now iface peer ts
    now=$(date +%s)
    for iface in $(wg show interfaces 2>/dev/null); do
        while IFS=$'\t ' read -r peer ts; do
            [ -z "$peer" ] && continue
            [ "$ts" != "0" ] && [ "$ts" -le "$now" ] && [ $((now - ts)) -lt 3600 ] && return 0
        done < <(wg show "$iface" latest-handshakes 2>/dev/null)
    done
    return 1
}

# docker-compute: USABLE docker (daemon up AND reachable by THIS user without sudo), never mere
# presence. `docker info` succeeds only when the socket is reachable — the ilya(in docker-group →
# usable) vs IdeaPad(installed but group-gated → `docker info` permission-denied) distinction. A
# real, artifact-backed compute capability for opt-in discovery (capability-gaps-ilya-retriage
# 2026-06-26; no consumer auto-dispatches yet — declared = discoverable, not a wired worker pool).
# timeout-guarded so a wedged daemon can't hang the refresh.
_docker_usable() { command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1 && timeout 4 docker info >/dev/null 2>&1; }

# ── BOUND EVERY ORGAN PROBE, AND SAY WHEN ONE DID NOT ANSWER ──────────────────────────────────────
# (2026-08-24, board task card-refresh-organ-probe-is-unbounded-and-its-tmp-is-unlocked, health@.)
# The refresh shelled `mesh-camera --test` and a dozen sibling probes with NO bound, so a spinning
# organ took the whole refresh — and the cron watchdog above it — hostage indefinitely: measured
# 1h42m of a burnt core plus a blind camera organ. It was INVISIBLE by construction, because
# mesh-card-watchdog is silent on rc=0 and a tick that never returns never writes the line that
# would show it hung; only _docker_usable above had a timeout, and it had it for exactly this reason.
# A PROBE THAT DID NOT ANSWER IS NOT AN ORGAN THAT ANSWERED NO. Folding a timeout into the absent
# branch makes a wedged webcam read exactly like a node with no camera; folding it into the present
# branch declares an organ on no evidence at all. Both are the silent-fallback shape CLAUDE.md
# refuses. So a timeout gets its own word — UNKNOWN — its own line on the card, and it never mints a
# roster entry (a probe that never answered is not a first declaration).
# The list has to cross a subshell boundary ($(detect_senses) runs in one), so it lives in a
# per-pid file rather than a variable a subshell would drop on the floor.
PROBE_UNKNOWN="${MESH_CARD_PROBE_UNKNOWN:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/.mesh-card-probe-unknown.$$}"
PROBE_SECS="${MESH_CARD_PROBE_SECS:-8}"
_probe_mark_unknown() { printf '%s\n' "$1" >> "$PROBE_UNKNOWN" 2>/dev/null || true; }
# _probe <organ> <cmd…> — boolean probe. rc 0 = the organ answered YES. A timeout returns non-zero
# (so nothing is declared on no evidence) AND is recorded, so it can never pass for a plain NO.
_probe() {
    local organ="$1" rc; shift
    timeout -k 2 "$PROBE_SECS" "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?
    case $rc in 124|137) _probe_mark_unknown "$organ"; return 1 ;; esac
    return $rc
}
# _probe_out <organ> <cmd…> — same bound for a probe whose OUTPUT is the evidence (the caller pipes
# it into grep/awk). On timeout the output is empty, so the caller's test fails closed — and the
# organ is still NAMED, which is the whole difference between failing closed and failing silent.
_probe_out() {
    local organ="$1" out rc; shift
    out="$(timeout -k 2 "$PROBE_SECS" "$@" 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
    printf '%s' "$out"
    case $rc in 124|137) _probe_mark_unknown "$organ"; return 1 ;; esac
    return $rc
}
_probe_unknown() { [ -s "$PROBE_UNKNOWN" ] && sort -u "$PROBE_UNKNOWN" | paste -sd' ' -; }
detect_actuators() {
    local a=""
    _probe_out speaker aplay -l | grep -q '^card' && a="$a speaker"
    _docker_usable && a="$a docker-compute"
    command -v termux-tts-speak >/dev/null 2>&1 && a="$a tts"
    command -v termux-sms-send  >/dev/null 2>&1 && a="$a sms"
    command -v mesh-say >/dev/null 2>&1 && _probe_out voice-out:bose aplay -l | grep -qi bose && a="$a voice-out:bose"
    # notify-send needs both DBUS and a graphical DISPLAY — without DISPLAY, it fails (Cannot autolaunch D-Bus / no daemon)
    command -v notify-send >/dev/null 2>&1 && [ -S "/run/user/$(id -u)/bus" ] && { [ -n "${DISPLAY-}" ] || [ -n "${WAYLAND_DISPLAY-}" ]; } && _probe notify dbus-send --bus="unix:path=/run/user/$(id -u)/bus" --dest=org.freedesktop.Notifications --type=method_call --print-reply /org/freedesktop/Notifications org.freedesktop.Notifications.GetServerInformation && a="$a notify"
    command -v mesh-volume >/dev/null 2>&1 && _probe volume mesh-volume --test && a="$a volume"
    command -v mesh-tv-dlna >/dev/null 2>&1 && command -v yt-dlp >/dev/null 2>&1 && a="$a tv dlna-tv"
    # speech/TTS actuator (spd-say, espeak-ng, festival, say) — voice output capability
    command -v spd-say >/dev/null 2>&1 && a="$a speech"
    command -v espeak-ng >/dev/null 2>&1 && a="$a speech"
    command -v festival >/dev/null 2>&1 && a="$a speech"
    command -v say >/dev/null 2>&1 && a="$a speech"
    # VPN/proxy services (actuators: this node runs them)
    command -v ss-server >/dev/null 2>&1 && a="$a shadowsocks"
    _wg_has_active_peers && a="$a wireguard-vpn"
    # self-describing capabilities (operator design 2026-06-21): each executable in ~/.mesh/caps/
    # is a capability this node OFFERS, named by its FILENAME. Surface the names so peers discover
    # + invoke them via mesh-organ (card-declared = public). The command/creds stay inside the file.
    local capd="${MESH_CAP_DIR:-$HOME/.mesh/caps}" n
    if [ -d "$capd" ]; then
        for n in "$capd"/*; do
            [ -x "$n" ] || continue
            n="${n##*/}"
            case " $a " in *" $n "*) ;; *) a="$a $n" ;; esac
        done
    fi
    echo "${a# }"
}

detect_connectivity() {
    local c=""
    # exit-node: this node consumes another node's egress
    _probe_out exit-node tailscale status --json | python3 -c "
import json,sys
try: d=json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception: sys.exit()
eid=(d.get('Self',{}) or {}).get('ExitNodeStatus') or {}
if eid: print('exit-node')
" 2>/dev/null | grep -q exit-node && c="$c exit-node"
    # public-ingress: this node offers public ingress (ngrok, bore, etc.)
    # Check for known ingress services running
    _probe_out public-ingress systemctl --user list-units --type=service --state=active --no-legend \
      | awk '{print $1}' | grep -qiE 'ngrok|bore|proxy-bot|mtg' && c="$c public-ingress"
    # exit-node-offered: this node OFFERS egress to others (advertised exit-node)
    _probe_out exit-node-offered tailscale status --peers=false | grep -q 'offers exit node' && c="$c exit-node-offered"
    # VPN/proxy services (connectivity: this node provides these tunnels)
    ( systemctl is-active shadowsocks-libev-server@config >/dev/null 2>&1 || systemctl is-active shadowsocks-libev >/dev/null 2>&1 ) && c="$c shadowsocks"
    _wg_has_active_peers && c="$c wireguard"
    echo "${c# }"
}

# --- health probes (best-effort — skip if tool absent) -------------------
health_power() {
    local raw source pct
    raw=$(mesh-power 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    source=$(echo "$raw" | awk '{print $2}')
    pct=$(echo "$raw" | grep -oP 'capacity=\K\d+')
    # battery-less nodes (VM/desktop: no capacity= field) → $pct empty → emit source only,
    # never a bare 'MAINS %' (stray % with no number) in the card / mesh-fleet-health POWER col.
    [ -n "$pct" ] && echo "${source} ${pct}%" || echo "${source}"
}

health_vitals() {
    local line distress rc
    line=$(mesh-vitals 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
    if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "OK $line"
    else
        distress=$(mesh-vitals --check 2>&1 || true)
        echo "DISTRESS $line ($distress)"
    fi
}

health_upstream() {
    local verdict
    verdict=$(mesh-isp-health --verdict 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    case "$verdict" in
        upstream-ok)      echo "ok" ;;
        upstream-down)    echo "down" ;;
        upstream-unknown) echo "unknown (no probe data)" ;;
        *) echo "$verdict" ;;
    esac
}

# probe_services — the mesh-relevant userland services this node is running, or '?' if we could not
# ask. Desktop/session noise is irrelevant here, hence the filter.
# Ask the bus SEPARATELY and keep its rc: piping systemctl straight into awk destroys it (the
# pipeline's rc is paste's, always 0 — which is what made the old `|| echo ''` dead code), so a
# bus-less run ("Failed to connect to bus: No medium found", rc=1) came back empty and rendered as
# `services-active: none` — a positive CLAIM that nothing mesh is running, from a probe that never
# got to ask. By hand it works; from cron/systemd (no XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS) it
# silently lied, and rc=0 either way so nothing downstream could tell the two apart.
# '?' is this file's own idiom for could-not-ask (:330 tsip, :339 pubip, :357 peer); 'none' stays
# reserved for the bus answering with zero matches, which is a real answer about the world.
# (chat-review/card-services-active-renders-cannot-ask-as-none, verified health@2026-07-15 23:17.)
probe_services() {
    local units out
    units=$(systemctl --user list-units --type=service --state=active --no-legend 2>/dev/null) || {
        printf '?\n'; return
    }
    out=$(printf '%s\n' "$units" | awk '{print $1}' \
          | grep -iE 'ngrok|bore|proxy-bot|vpn-hub|mtg|mesh' | paste -sd' ' -)
    printf '%s\n' "${out:-none}"
}

# --- exit-node LAN exclusion: ask the FIB, NEVER the pref ---------------------
# A DECLARED PREF IS NOT THE FIB. Measured live on mesh-home 2026-08-21T10:50Z (health@, board
# [fyi]): with phaedra set as exit node, `tailscale debug prefs` read `ExitNodeAllowLANAccess:
# true` and the LAN was swallowed anyway — `ip route get 192.168.8.1` answered
# `dev tailscale0 table 52`, and table 52 held a default plus nine 100.x peers with ZERO
# 192.168 entries and ZERO throw routes. The exclusion the pref promises was never installed,
# and tailscaled had NRestarts=0 (up 25h), so no `tailscale up` had reset it: the pref and its
# implementation simply diverged.
#
# WHY IT BELONGS ON THE CARD. It is the documented substrate invariant ("the forwarding mark
# must exclude LAN/private ranges") being violated by the exit-node path, and it is SILENT in
# the one direction anybody watches: egress stayed perfect throughout (public IP correct,
# tailscale peers=7), so every OUTWARD probe was green while the node was blind INWARD. Five
# organs alarmed at once — router LAN-ping OFF, router-thermal unreachable, sim UNREACHABLE,
# mesh path-down-egress — and each named its own far end as the fault. L2 was healthy the whole
# time (`ip neigh` REACHABLE, wifi -46 dBm): ARP answers prove our frames reach the LAN and the
# LAN replies; only IPv4 forwarding died.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT READ THE PREF. CLAUDE.local.md's remedy for this exact failure ("set
# --exit-node-allow-lan-access=true; re-check it after ANY tailscale up; verify by pinging a
# LAN peer") is refuted by that measurement — it was already true. This is the mode-bit trap
# one ring out: `[ -w ]` is not what the kernel will DO with your write, and a pref is not what
# the kernel will DO with your packet. So the probe asks the FIB the same question the LAN
# asks, and the verdict is the DEVICE that comes back.
#
# lan_probe_target <cidr> -> "<addr> <network/prefix>", or empty.
# The address must NOT be our own: `ip rule` 0 (`from all lookup local`) precedes the tailscale
# rule, so our own address answers `local` and proves nothing about the LAN.
lan_probe_target() {
    [ -n "${1:-}" ] || return 0
    python3 - "$1" <<'PY' 2>/dev/null
import ipaddress, sys
try:
    iface = ipaddress.ip_interface(sys.argv[1])
except Exception:
    sys.exit()
net = iface.network
if net.prefixlen >= 31:      # /31,/32 hold no third address to probe with
    sys.exit()
for cand in (net.network_address + 1, net.network_address + 2):
    if cand != iface.ip and cand in net:
        print(cand, net); break
PY
}

# fib_route_dev / fib_route_table <route-get-output> -> the DEVICE / TABLE the FIB named.
# fib_swallowed <dev> -> TRUE (rc 0) if that device is the exit-node tunnel.
#
# THE DECIDING GRAMMAR LIVES HERE, ONCE. Two subjects now ask the FIB the same question — the LAN
# prefix (lan_fib_verdict) and the docker bridge (docker_bridge_kv) — and a second private copy of
# "is this device the tunnel?" is the shape where n copies are each green and none is complete.
# The renderers below differ (different nouns, different remedies); the predicate does not.
fib_route_dev()   { printf '%s\n' "${1:-}" | grep -oP '\bdev \K\S+'   | head -1; }
fib_route_table() { printf '%s\n' "${1:-}" | grep -oP '\btable \K\S+' | head -1; }
fib_swallowed()   { case "${1:-}" in tailscale*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }

# lan_fib_verdict <exit-node> <target> <network> <route-get-output> -> rendered verdict.
# PURE — every input is passed in, so --test drives it with fixtures instead of the live node.
# rc0 = ok or an honest n/a; rc2 = the LAN is swallowed.
lan_fib_verdict() {
    local xnode="$1" target="$2" net="$3" route="$4" dev table
    case "${xnode:-}" in
        '' | none) echo "n/a (no exit node — nothing can swallow the LAN)"; return 0 ;;
        '?')       echo "n/a (could not ask tailscale for the exit node)"; return 0 ;;
    esac
    [ -n "$target" ] || { echo "n/a (no usable LAN prefix on this node)"; return 0; }
    [ -n "$route" ]  || { echo "n/a (FIB lookup for $target returned nothing)"; return 0; }
    dev=$(fib_route_dev "$route")
    [ -n "$dev" ] || { echo "n/a (FIB answer for $target names no device: $route)"; return 0; }
    if fib_swallowed "$dev"; then
        table=$(fib_route_table "$route")
        echo "⚠ SWALLOWED — $target routes via dev $dev${table:+ table $table}, not the LAN link, while exit-node '$xnode' carries egress. Inward LAN sight is dead and every outward probe stays green. The pref is NOT the fix (measured true during the fault, mesh-home 2026-08-21T10:50Z) — install the exclusion in the FIB: ip route add throw $net table ${table:-<t>}"
        return 2
    fi
    echo "ok ($target -> dev $dev, exit-node '$xnode')"; return 0
}

# probe_exit_node -> the exit node this node CONSUMES, by hostname; "none" | "?" on an unreadable ask.
# Extracted from refresh() so the standalone `--exit-node-lan` probe below asks the SAME question the
# card asks. A second inline copy would be a second definition of one fact, and the two would drift.
probe_exit_node() {
    tailscale status --json 2>/dev/null | python3 -c "
import json,sys
try: d=json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception: print('?'); sys.exit()
eid=(d.get('Self',{}) or {}).get('ExitNodeStatus') or {}
# resolve ExitNodeID from prefs
import subprocess
pj=subprocess.run(['tailscale','debug','prefs'],capture_output=True,text=True).stdout
try: p=json.loads(pj)
except Exception: p={}
xid=p.get('ExitNodeID') or ''
if not xid: print('none'); sys.exit()
for peer in (d.get('Peer') or {}).values():
    if peer.get('ID')==xid: print(peer.get('HostName',xid)); sys.exit()
print(xid)
" 2>/dev/null || echo '?'
}

# lan_own_cidr -> this node's own LAN address/prefix, or empty. THE ONE definition (refresh() calls it).
#
# WHY THE DEFAULT-ROUTE DEVICE, NOT head -1 OF THE ADDRESS LIST. Until 2026-08-22 this took the
# first global v4 address that was not lo/tailscale/docker/bridge/veth/wg — and `ip -o -4 addr` is
# ifindex order, which is PLUG order, not importance order. The operator's Note 3 came up as a USB
# tether (`enx0257356d3666`, 192.168.42.130/24, ifindex 3) and outranked the actual LAN
# (`wlxbcec43434a22`, 192.168.8.224/24, ifindex 5) simply by being enumerated first. The probe then
# measured 192.168.42.1 — a network with its own table 42 and its own default — and answered
# `ok (192.168.42.1 -> dev wlxbcec43434a22)`, i.e. a GREEN verdict about a prefix nobody was
# defending, while 192.168.8.0/24, the one the exit node swallows, was not asked about at all.
#
# The damage is on both legs of the loop, and neither is loud: the card's `exit-node-lan:` line (and
# `invariant-check`, and `mesh-card-watchdog`) reads permanently ok for the wrong subject, and
# `mesh-exit-node-lan-heal` — which takes NET and TABLE from these fields — would re-install
# `throw 192.168.42.0/24` and leave the real LAN dead. Same shape one ring out from the case this
# probe was built for: a pref is not the FIB, and here the FIB was asked a question about the wrong
# network. Bind the subject to the route the node actually reaches its LAN over.
#
# The old ifindex read stays as the FALLBACK for a node with no default route (or one whose default
# rides an overlay), because an empty cidr renders `n/a (no usable LAN prefix)` — an honest blind,
# but a blind, and on a node with exactly one physical link the old read was already correct.
lan_own_cidr() {
    local dev cidr
    dev=$(ip -4 route show default 2>/dev/null | grep -oP '\bdev \K\S+' | head -1)
    case "${dev:-}" in
        lo|tailscale*|docker*|br-*|veth*|wg*|Gtcld*) dev='' ;;
    esac
    if [ -n "${dev:-}" ]; then
        cidr=$(ip -o -4 addr show dev "$dev" scope global 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $4}' | head -1)
        [ -n "$cidr" ] && { printf '%s\n' "$cidr"; return 0; }
    fi
    ip -o -4 addr show scope global 2>/dev/null \
      | awk '$2!~/^(lo|tailscale|docker|br-|veth|wg|Gtcld)/ {print $4}' | head -1
}

# exit_node_lan_kv -> the exit-node LAN leg ALONE, as machine-readable key: value lines, rc 0/2.
#
# WHY A STANDALONE SUBCOMMAND. The card renders this verdict, and `mesh-card-watchdog` alarms on it
# through invariant-check — but detection closes the loop only if a hand is watching the board. The
# re-application leg (`mesh-exit-node-lan-heal`) needs the same fact on a per-minute cadence, and a
# full `--refresh` is the wrong price for it (curl to ifconfig.me, systemd bus walk, the whole card
# rewritten) AND the wrong shape (an actuator must not parse prose out of a rendered card). So the
# leg gets its own entry point: same probe target, same `lan_fib_verdict`, no second definition of
# the fact — and it emits the NET and TABLE as fields, so the healer never regexes them back out of
# an English sentence that is free to be reworded.
#
# rc mirrors lan_fib_verdict: 2 = SWALLOWED, 0 = ok or an honest n/a. `state:` says which, because
# an rc alone cannot separate "the LAN is fine" from "there is no exit node to swallow it".
exit_node_lan_kv() {
    local cidr xnode target net route verdict rc dev table state
    cidr=$(lan_own_cidr)
    xnode=$(probe_exit_node)
    read -r target net <<<"$(lan_probe_target "${cidr:-}")"
    route=""
    [ -n "${target:-}" ] && route=$(ip route get "$target" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
    verdict=$(lan_fib_verdict "$xnode" "${target:-}" "${net:-}" "$route"); rc=$?
    dev=$(printf '%s\n' "$route" | grep -oP '\bdev \K\S+' | head -1)
    table=$(printf '%s\n' "$route" | grep -oP '\btable \K\S+' | head -1)
    case "$rc" in 2) state=swallowed ;; *) case "$verdict" in 'n/a'*) state=na ;; *) state=ok ;; esac ;; esac
    echo "state: $state"
    echo "exit-node: ${xnode:-?}"
    echo "own-cidr: ${cidr:-}"
    echo "target: ${target:-}"
    echo "net: ${net:-}"
    echo "dev: ${dev:-}"
    echo "table: ${table:-}"
    echo "verdict: $verdict"
    return $rc
}

refresh() {
    local host tsip lanip lancidr egress_dev wg_ifaces fwd pubip exitnode svc inv_check wg_check
    # a fresh UNKNOWN list per refresh — a probe that timed out an hour ago is not a fact about now
    rm -f "$PROBE_UNKNOWN" 2>/dev/null || true
    local lan_target lan_net lan_route lan_check lan_rc _pu
    host=$(hostname)
    tsip=$(tailscale ip -4 2>/dev/null | head -1 || echo '?')
    # real LAN IP — the ONE picker (lan_own_cidr), never a second inline copy. This WAS an inline
    # duplicate of the old ifindex-order read, exactly the drift the comment above probe_exit_node
    # warns about: when the picker was fixed to follow the default route, this copy would have kept
    # the card's own lan: line on the tether while --exit-node-lan moved to the LAN.
    lancidr=$(lan_own_cidr)
    lanip=${lancidr%%/*}
    egress_dev=$(ip route get 1.1.1.1 2>/dev/null | grep -oP 'dev \K\S+' | head -1)
    # wireguard interfaces present (no sudo needed)
    wg_ifaces=$(ip -o link show type wireguard 2>/dev/null | awk -F': ' '{print $2}' | cut -d@ -f1 | paste -sd' ' -)
    fwd=$(cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward 2>/dev/null)
    pubip=$(curl -s --max-time 6 https://ifconfig.me 2>/dev/null || echo '?')

    # exit-node (this node consuming someone else's egress)
    exitnode=$(probe_exit_node)

    svc=$(probe_services)

    # exit-node LAN exclusion, measured in the FIB (see lan_fib_verdict above — never the pref)
    read -r lan_target lan_net <<<"$(lan_probe_target "${lancidr:-}")"
    lan_route=""
    [ -n "${lan_target:-}" ] && lan_route=$(ip route get "$lan_target" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
    lan_check=$(lan_fib_verdict "$exitnode" "${lan_target:-}" "${lan_net:-}" "$lan_route"); lan_rc=$?

    # --- INVARIANT CHECK: is this node's OWN control plane riding a local VPN? ---
    # TWO legs, and the rollup must never speak for a leg that did not fire (a summary line that
    # asserts legs it never ran is its own failure shape). `wg_check` stays the WireGuard leg
    # alone, because the `default-egress:` line below renders "⚠ via WireGuard" off it — keyed on
    # the rollup, that suffix would libel a perfectly clean egress the moment the LAN leg tripped.
    wg_check="OK"
    if [ -n "$egress_dev" ] && printf ' %s ' "$wg_ifaces" | grep -q " $egress_dev "; then
        wg_check="⚠ VIOLATED — host's own default egress is dev $egress_dev (a local WireGuard tunnel). The control plane is betting on an offered route. One endpoint flap = lockout. Set Table=off + scope to forwarded traffic only."
    fi
    inv_check="$wg_check"
    # Same invariant, the CONSUMING side: a route this node rides must not carry its own LAN.
    # Folded into the rollup so the existing watchdog/alert path carries it — a new violation
    # class must not need a new alarm nobody wired. Appended, never substituted: both legs can
    # be violated at once and the second must not erase the first.
    if [ "$lan_rc" = 2 ]; then
        if [ "$inv_check" = OK ]; then inv_check="$lan_check"
        else inv_check="$inv_check  ALSO: $lan_check"; fi
    fi

    local invariant notes
    invariant=$(extract_invariant)
    notes=$(extract_notes)

    {
        echo "hostname: $host"
        echo "tailscale-ip: $tsip"
        echo "lan-ip: ${lanip:-?}"
        echo "as-of: $(ts)  (live-refreshed)"
        echo
        echo "invariant:"
        printf '%s\n' "$invariant"
        echo
        echo "live (auto — regenerated by \`mesh-card --refresh\`; do not hand-edit):"
        echo "  default-egress: dev ${egress_dev:-?}$([ "$wg_check" = OK ] && echo '  (clean)' || echo '  ⚠ via WireGuard')"
        echo "  public-ip: $pubip"
        echo "  exit-node: $exitnode"
        echo "  exit-node-lan: $lan_check"
        echo "  ip-forwarding: $([ "$fwd" = 1 ] && echo on || echo off)"
        echo "  wireguard-ifaces: ${wg_ifaces:-none}"
        # bare $svc, not ${svc:-none}: the none/?/list decision is made ONCE at the probe above, where
        # the bus rc is still readable. A :- default here would be a second, blinder definition of the
        # same thing — and the one that re-renders "none" the moment the probe branch breaks.
        echo "  services-active: $svc"
        echo "  invariant-check: $inv_check"
        # S5 identity coherence (VSM PII2). Report-only and deliberately IN the card: the card is
        # the identity surface every other tool trusts, so the count of rival conceptions belongs
        # where those tools already look — including peers reading this node's card.
        echo "  identity-coherence: $(identity_verdict)"
        echo
        echo "health:"
        local hp hv hu
        command -v mesh-power >/dev/null 2>&1 && hp=$(health_power 2>/dev/null) || hp=""
        command -v mesh-vitals >/dev/null 2>&1 && hv=$(health_vitals 2>/dev/null) || hv=""
        command -v mesh-isp-health >/dev/null 2>&1 && hu=$(health_upstream 2>/dev/null) || hu=""
        [ -n "$hp" ] && echo "  power: $hp"
        [ -n "$hv" ] && echo "  vitals: $hv"
        [ -n "$hu" ] && echo "  upstream: $hu"
        echo
        echo "capabilities:"
        echo "  minds: $(detect_minds | sed 's/^$/(none)/')"
        # Detected ∪ roster − retired: a probe that goes false moves an organ to DARK in keepalive,
        # it never shrinks the card's TOTAL (organ-roster-shrink-hides-a-dead-organ).
        echo "  senses: $(roster_declared sense $(detect_senses) | sed 's/^$/(none)/')"
        echo "  actuators: $(roster_declared actuator $(detect_actuators) | sed 's/^$/(none)/')"
        echo "  connectivity: $(detect_connectivity | sed 's/^$/(none)/')"
        # ORGANS THAT DID NOT ANSWER. Rendered only when non-empty, and deliberately NOT folded into
        # the three lines above: those say what this node IS, and a probe that timed out says
        # nothing about that either way. Absent line = every probe answered.
        _pu="$(_probe_unknown)"
        [ -n "$_pu" ] && echo "  organs-unknown: $_pu (probe exceeded ${PROBE_SECS}s — neither present nor absent)"
        echo
        echo "known-peers:"
        # Use mesh-peers (collision-deduped): Tailscale keeps a STALE duplicate when a node
        # re-registers (same HostName, old IP, offline) → a naive scan shows a LIVE node OFFLINE
        # in the durable card. mesh-peers collapses each HostName to its best (online) record.
        # Inline fallback keeps the card working on a fresh node before mesh-peers is deployed.
        if command -v mesh-peers >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            # --verify: corroborate liveness with a fresh ping so the DURABLE card never records a
            # frozen-but-Online peer as 'online' (the cached-card-status lie — operator 2026-06-21).
            # A peer Tailscale calls Online but that fails the ping is written 'stale', not 'online';
            # and one Tailscale calls offline that ANSWERS on its resolved LAN address is written
            # 'unreach' + the LAN address, never 'offline' (which claims the device is not there).
            mesh-peers --json --verify 2>/dev/null | python3 -c "
import json,sys
try: rows=json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception: sys.exit()
for r in rows:
    st=r.get('state') or ('online' if r.get('online') else 'offline')
    # state=unreach means present on the LAN but not enumerable over the tailnet. The row's ip stays
    # the TAILSCALE address (that is the identity the tailnet knows it by, dead or not), so the
    # address that actually answered must be NAMED beside it or the card records a presence claim
    # nobody can act on. mesh-peers sets lan_ip only from an address it re-pinged.
    lan=r.get('lan_ip') or ''
    print(f\"  {r['hostname']}: {r['ip']} ({r['os']}, {st}{', lan ' + lan if lan else ''})\")
" 2>/dev/null
        else
            tailscale status --json 2>/dev/null | python3 -c "
import json,sys
try: d=json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception: sys.exit()
for p in (d.get('Peer') or {}).values():
    if 'tag:lte-node' in (p.get('Tags') or []):
        st='online' if p.get('Online') else 'offline'
        print(f\"  {p.get('HostName','?')}: {p['TailscaleIPs'][0]} ({p.get('OS','?')}, {st})\")
" 2>/dev/null
        fi
        echo
        [ -n "$notes" ] && printf '%s\n' "$notes" || { echo "notes:"; echo "  - (none yet)"; }
    # PER-PID TMP (2026-08-24, same board task). This was the fixed path "$CARD.tmp" with no lock,
    # so two concurrent refreshes interleaved INTO THE SAME FILE and the mv published the splice:
    # measured 22:06Z, a ~/.mesh-card carrying known-peers TWICE with the header fused to the first
    # peer row — the DURABLE self-description tier corrupted, and self-repairing on the next clean
    # refresh, so it leaves no trace unless someone reads the file inside the window. Per-pid tmp +
    # atomic same-directory mv makes the two writers independent and the publish indivisible: the
    # loser's card is simply overwritten by the winner's, which is a whole card either way.
    } > "$CARD.tmp.$$" && mv "$CARD.tmp.$$" "$CARD" || { rm -f "$CARD.tmp.$$" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }

    cat "$CARD"
    if [ "$inv_check" != OK ]; then
        echo >&2
        echo "mesh-card: INVARIANT VIOLATION detected (see invariant-check above)." >&2
        return 2
    fi
}

case "${1:-}" in
    --refresh) refresh ;;
    --exit-node-lan) exit_node_lan_kv ;;
    --roster)  # the sticky organ roster: what this node has ever declared, and what was retired.
               echo "=== organ roster ($ORGAN_ROSTER) ==="
               if [ -s "$ORGAN_ROSTER" ]; then
                   while read -r _c _o _t; do
                       [ -n "${_o:-}" ] || continue
                       if _organ_retired "$_o"; then echo "  $_c $_o  (first-declared $_t) — RETIRED"
                       else echo "  $_c $_o  (first-declared $_t)"; fi
                   done < "$ORGAN_ROSTER"
               else echo "  (empty — no refresh has run yet)"; fi
               echo "retired ($ORGAN_RETIRED): $( [ -s "$ORGAN_RETIRED" ] && echo $(cat "$ORGAN_RETIRED") || echo '(none)')" ;;
    --retire-organ) # the ONLY way an organ leaves the card. Deliberate, logged, and reversible by
               # deleting the line from $ORGAN_RETIRED — the roster is monotone EXCEPT by intent.
               _o="${2:-}"; [ -n "$_o" ] || { echo "usage: mesh-card --retire-organ <organ>" >&2; exit 1; }
               mkdir -p "$(dirname "$ORGAN_RETIRED")" 2>/dev/null || true
               if _organ_retired "$_o"; then echo "mesh-card: '$_o' already retired"; else
                   printf '%s\n' "$_o" >> "$ORGAN_RETIRED"
                   echo "mesh-card: retired organ '$_o' — it will leave the card on the next --refresh"
                   command -v mesh-trace >/dev/null 2>&1 && \
                     mesh-trace "[organ-retire] $(hostname): '$_o' explicitly retired from the card roster" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
               fi ;;
    --identity) # full PII2 conflict list (the card carries only the count + the first line)
               out=$(identity_conflicts); rc=$?
               [ "$rc" = 2 ] && { echo "identity: n/a (no readable restore.env — cannot ask)"; exit 2; }
               if [ -z "$out" ]; then echo "identity: COHERENT (no rival declarations found)"; else
                 echo "identity: ⚠ CONFLICTED — VSM PII2 (institutional schizophrenia), $(printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -c .) conflict(s)"
                 printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/  - /'
               fi ;;
    --test)    failures=0
               for dep in tailscale python3 hostname ip; do
                 command -v "$dep" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL ($dep missing)"; failures=1; }
               done
               for dep in curl systemctl wg; do
                 command -v "$dep" >/dev/null 2>&1 || echo "smoke-test: WARN ($dep missing — feature degraded)"
               done
               echo '{"Self":{"HostName":"test"},"Peer":{}}' | python3 -c "import json,sys; json.load(sys.stdin)" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
                 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (python3 JSON parse failed)"; failures=1; }
               ip -4 route get 1.1.1.1 >/dev/null 2>&1 \
                 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (ip route failed)"; failures=1; }
               # docker-compute is declared IFF docker is USABLE (`docker info` succeeds), never on
               # mere presence — a group-gated docker (info → permission denied, the IdeaPad case) must
               # NOT declare. Stub docker in a scratch PATH: info→0 = usable, info→1 = group-gated.
               _dtd="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$_dtd/bin"
               printf '#!/bin/sh\ncase "$1" in info) exit 0;; *) exit 0;; esac\n' > "$_dtd/bin/docker"; chmod +x "$_dtd/bin/docker"
               PATH="$_dtd/bin:$PATH" _docker_usable \
                 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (usable docker: info succeeds but not detected)"; failures=1; }
               printf '#!/bin/sh\ncase "$1" in info) exit 1;; *) exit 0;; esac\n' > "$_dtd/bin/docker"
               PATH="$_dtd/bin:$PATH" _docker_usable \
                 && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (group-gated docker: info fails yet declared usable — the phantom-capability hazard)"; failures=1; }
               rm -rf "$_dtd"
               # minds_off_by_channels fixture contract (2026-07-08 chat-review: card must not
               # auto-un-blank minds: past a restore.env lean-allowlist off-switch)
               _mtd="$(mktemp -d)"; _me="$_mtd/restore.env"
               printf 'MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="none"\n' > "$_me"
               minds_off_by_channels "$_me" \
                 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (MESH_MIND_CHANNELS=none must read as off — the live ds/Default-string regression)"; failures=1; }
               printf 'MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="genome chat"\n' > "$_me"
               minds_off_by_channels "$_me" \
                 && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a real lean allowlist like 'genome chat' must NOT read as off — would blank phaedra)"; failures=1; }
               : > "$_me"
               minds_off_by_channels "$_me" \
                 && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unset MESH_MIND_CHANNELS = full uniform suite, must not read as off)"; failures=1; }
               rm -f "$_me"
               minds_off_by_channels "$_me" \
                 && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (missing/unreadable restore.env must not read as off)"; failures=1; }
               rm -rf "$_mtd"
               # services-active must distinguish "the bus said zero" from "I could not ask the bus"
               # (chat-review/card-services-active-renders-cannot-ask-as-none, verified health@23:17:
               # a cron/systemd refresh has no DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS, systemctl exits 1, and the
               # card claimed `services-active: none` on the mind node while 4 mesh services ran).
               # Falsify: pipe systemctl straight into awk (throwing away its rc) or restore
               # `${svc:-none}` at the render → arm (a) and arm (d) both go back to "none" → FAIL.
               # Shims live in $_std/.local/bin and arm (d) runs with HOME=$_std — NOT a plain PATH
               # prepend. :74 does `export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"`, which the `$0 --refresh`
               # child re-runs, putting the REAL ~/.local/bin ahead of any dir the parent prepended:
               # a PATH-shimmed child silently ran the live tools (the sense probes still took 6.5s
               # apiece, and only `systemctl` shimmed correctly — by luck, because ~/.local/bin has
               # no systemctl). Anchoring HOME puts the shims where :74 itself will find them.
               _std="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$_std/.local/bin"
               _sbin="$_std/.local/bin"
               _fake_systemctl() { printf '%s\n' "$1" > "$_sbin/systemctl"; chmod +x "$_sbin/systemctl"; }
               _BUSLESS='#!/bin/sh
echo "Failed to connect to bus: No medium found" >&2
exit 1'
               # (a) bus unreachable — exactly what cron/systemd sees. rc=1 + the real stderr.
               _fake_systemctl "$_BUSLESS"
               _got="$(PATH="$_sbin:$PATH" probe_services)"
               [ "$_got" = '?' ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (bus-less probe → '$_got' — must be '?', never a claim about what is running)"; failures=1; }
               # (b) bus answers, genuinely zero mesh services — "none" is a REAL answer, keep it.
               # (This is the arm that makes the find's prescribed `${svc:-?}` wrong: it would render
               # '?' on a node that truly runs none, trading one lie for its mirror image.)
               _fake_systemctl '#!/bin/sh
echo "  cups.service loaded active running CUPS Scheduler"
exit 0'
               _got="$(PATH="$_sbin:$PATH" probe_services)"
               [ "$_got" = none ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (bus answered with no mesh units → '$_got', must be 'none')"; failures=1; }
               # (c) bus answers with mesh units — they must survive the filter
               _fake_systemctl '#!/bin/sh
echo "  mesh-overhear.service loaded active running overhear"
echo "  cups.service loaded active running CUPS Scheduler"
exit 0'
               _got="$(PATH="$_sbin:$PATH" probe_services)"
               [ "$_got" = mesh-overhear.service ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (live mesh unit → '$_got')"; failures=1; }
               # (d) and the probe must actually be WIRED INTO THE CARD — (a)-(c) only prove the
               # function decides right, which is exactly the "hand-run of its innards" health warned
               # against. One REAL refresh through the real writer, bus-less, asserting the rendered
               # line.
               # Only one, and shimmed: an unshimmed refresh is 11.5-22s and VARIES, because its
               # sense probes SSH to phones — so this arm's runtime would track phone latency and
               # eventually blow the `timeout 30` that doctor/autowire impose, reading as a failure
               # of a tool that is fine. The shims below are the network (curl/tailscale) and the
               # five `mesh-* --test` sense probes, which exit 1 → that sense is simply not listed.
               # Nothing shimmed here is anything arm (d) asserts: it reads only services-active,
               # and systemctl — the thing under test — stays real.
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_sbin/curl"
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_sbin/tailscale"
               for _p in mesh-camera mesh-body-motion mesh-light mesh-link-flap mesh-volume; do
                   printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 1\n' > "$_sbin/$_p"
               done
               chmod +x "$_sbin"/*
               _fake_systemctl "$_BUSLESS"
               HOME="$_std" MESH_CARD="$_std/card" "$SELF" --refresh >/dev/null 2>&1
               _got="$(sed -n 's/^  services-active: //p' "$_std/card")"
               [ "$_got" = '?' ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (bus-less REFRESH rendered 'services-active: $_got' — the probe is right but the card does not use it)"; failures=1; }
               rm -rf "$_std"
               # --- BOUNDED ORGAN PROBES + AN UNSHARED TMP ----------------------------------
               # (2026-08-24, board task card-refresh-organ-probe-is-unbounded-and-its-tmp-is-
               # unlocked.) Two independent defects, two arms, and both drive the REAL writer —
               # the hazard is in the refresh, not in a function held at arm's length.
               # Shims live in a FAKE HOME's .local/bin for the reason spelled out above: :76
               # re-prepends the real ~/.local/bin, so a plain PATH prepend loses the race.
               _ptd="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$_ptd/.local/bin"
               _pbin="$_ptd/.local/bin"
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_pbin/curl"
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_pbin/tailscale"
               for _p in mesh-body-motion mesh-light mesh-link-flap mesh-volume; do
                   printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 1\n' > "$_pbin/$_p"
               done
               # (P1) THE SPINNING ORGAN. This is the measured fault: an organ probe that never
               # returns took the refresh AND the cron watchdog above it hostage for 1h42m. The
               # outer bound is the assertion — if the refresh does not come back on its own, the
               # arm reports the hang instead of hanging the suite. Drive it RED by calling the
               # probe directly instead of through the bounded helper: the refresh never returns.
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$_pbin/mesh-camera"
               chmod +x "$_pbin"/*
               HOME="$_ptd" MESH_CARD="$_ptd/card" MESH_CARD_PROBE_SECS=2 \
                 timeout 45 "$SELF" --refresh >/dev/null 2>&1
               [ "$?" = 124 ] && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a spinning organ probe hung the whole refresh — the bound is gone; this is the 1h42m fault)"; failures=1; }
               [ -s "$_ptd/card" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (bounded refresh produced no card at all)"; failures=1; }
               # ...and the organ that did not answer must be NAMED. A timeout folded into the
               # absent branch is indistinguishable from a node with no camera at all.
               grep -q '^  organs-unknown:.*camera' "$_ptd/card" \
                 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a timed-out organ probe left no mark — it reads exactly like an organ that answered NO)"; sed -n '/^capabilities:/,/^$/p' "$_ptd/card" | sed 's/^/    /'; failures=1; }
               # ...and it must NOT be declared present, nor admitted to the sticky roster: a probe
               # that never answered is not a first declaration (that is how a phantom organ is
               # minted for good, since the roster is monotone).
               sed -n 's/^  senses: //p' "$_ptd/card" | grep -qw camera \
                 && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (an organ that never answered was DECLARED present)"; failures=1; }
               grep -q '^sense camera' "$_ptd/.mesh/organ-roster" 2>/dev/null \
                 && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a timed-out probe minted a sticky roster entry — monotone, so it is permanent)"; failures=1; }
               # (P2) THE SHARED TMP. The card went to a FIXED $CARD.tmp with no lock, so a
               # second refresh opened — and truncated — the very file the first was still writing,
               # then moved it out from under it: measured live 22:06Z as a ~/.mesh-card carrying
               # known-peers TWICE with the header fused to the first peer row, i.e. the DURABLE
               # self-description tier corrupted, self-repairing on the next clean refresh so it
               # leaves no trace unless someone reads the file inside the window.
               # THE ASSERTION IS THE COLLISION ITSELF, not the odds of a visible splice. Two
               # refreshes racing at the same speed write near-identical bytes and usually publish
               # something that READS fine — which is exactly why this went unseen — so asserting
               # "the card looks whole" would be a gate that never fails. Instead: stall writer A
               # mid-write, run writer B to completion, and while A is STILL holding its file,
               # require A's own in-progress file to be intact. Sharing one path makes that
               # impossible by construction; a per-pid path makes it certain.
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nif mkdir "$HOME/.cam-stall" 2>/dev/null; then sleep 6; fi\nexit 1\n' > "$_pbin/mesh-camera"
               chmod +x "$_pbin/mesh-camera"
               rm -f "$_ptd/card"; rm -rf "$_ptd/.cam-stall"
               HOME="$_ptd" MESH_CARD="$_ptd/card" MESH_CARD_PROBE_SECS=30 timeout 60 "$SELF" --refresh >/dev/null 2>&1 &
               _pa=$!
               sleep 2                                   # A is now inside the write, stalled on the organ
               _tmp_a="$(ls "$_ptd"/card.tmp* 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
               _sz_a="$(wc -c < "$_tmp_a" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
               [ "${_sz_a:-0}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null \
                 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (arm P2 asserts nothing: writer A was not mid-write when B started — no in-progress tmp with content)"; failures=1; }
               HOME="$_ptd" MESH_CARD="$_ptd/card" MESH_CARD_PROBE_SECS=30 timeout 60 "$SELF" --refresh >/dev/null 2>&1
               _sz_a2="$(wc -c < "$_tmp_a" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
               [ "${_sz_a2:-0}" -ge "${_sz_a:-0}" ] 2>/dev/null \
                 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a second refresh truncated or removed the file the first was still writing — the two writers share one tmp path; this is the 22:06Z card corruption)"; failures=1; }
               wait "$_pa" 2>/dev/null
               # and the finished pair must leave ONE whole card and no debris.
               for _sec in '^known-peers:' '^capabilities:' '^hostname:'; do
                   [ "$(grep -c "$_sec" "$_ptd/card" 2>/dev/null)" = 1 ] \
                     || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (concurrent refreshes left a card where '$_sec' appears $(grep -c "$_sec" "$_ptd/card" 2>/dev/null)x)"; failures=1; }
               done
               ls "$_ptd"/card.tmp* >/dev/null 2>&1 \
                 && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a refresh left its tmp behind: $(ls "$_ptd"/card.tmp* | tr '\n' ' '))"; failures=1; }
               rm -rf "$_ptd"
               # --- exit-node LAN exclusion: the FIB, never the pref -------------------------
               # Every arm drives the REAL lan_probe_target/lan_fib_verdict on fixtures, and arm
               # (L6) drives the REAL writer, because (L1)-(L5) only prove the functions decide
               # right. The live fault this was built from (health@, mesh-home 2026-08-21T10:50Z)
               # is a NODE condition, so it can never be an assertion here: arm (L1) carries it as
               # a fixture instead, and stays red-able on a node where the LAN is fine.
               _lfail(){ echo "smoke-test: FAIL (exit-node-lan: $1)"; failures=1; }
               _TS_ROUTE='192.168.8.1 dev tailscale0 table 52 src 100.81.222.19 uid 1000'
               _LAN_ROUTE='192.168.8.1 dev wlxbcec43434a22 src 192.168.8.224 uid 1000'
               # (L1) the swallowed LAN — an exit node is set and the FIB sends the LAN into it.
               _lout="$(lan_fib_verdict phaedra 192.168.8.1 192.168.8.0/24 "$_TS_ROUTE")"; _lrc=$?
               [ "$_lrc" = 2 ] || _lfail "a LAN routed via dev tailscale0 returned rc=$_lrc, must be 2"
               case "$_lout" in *SWALLOWED*) ;; *) _lfail "swallowed LAN rendered '$_lout'" ;; esac
               # and the remedy must name the REAL table it read, not a placeholder — a remedy that
               # cannot be pasted is a remedy nobody applies
               case "$_lout" in *"throw 192.168.8.0/24 table 52"*) ;; *) _lfail "remedy did not carry the measured net+table: '$_lout'" ;; esac
               # (L2) same exit node, LAN on its own link — the healthy case must NOT warn, or the
               # verdict is stuck accusing and stops carrying information
               _lout="$(lan_fib_verdict phaedra 192.168.8.1 192.168.8.0/24 "$_LAN_ROUTE")"; _lrc=$?
               [ "$_lrc" = 0 ] || _lfail "a LAN on its own link returned rc=$_lrc, must be 0"
               case "$_lout" in ok*) ;; *) _lfail "healthy LAN rendered '$_lout'" ;; esac
               # (L3) no exit node = nothing can swallow the LAN. n/a, never ok — the difference
               # between "I checked and it is fine" and "there was nothing to check".
               case "$(lan_fib_verdict none 192.168.8.1 192.168.8.0/24 "$_TS_ROUTE")" in
                 n/a*) ;; *) _lfail "no exit node must render n/a, got '$(lan_fib_verdict none 192.168.8.1 192.168.8.0/24 "$_TS_ROUTE")'" ;;
               esac
               lan_fib_verdict none 192.168.8.1 192.168.8.0/24 "$_TS_ROUTE" >/dev/null \
                 || _lfail "no exit node must not raise a violation rc"
               # (L4) missing evidence renders n/a, never a fabricated ok: an unaskable tailscale,
               # no LAN prefix, and an empty FIB answer are three different silences.
               for _lf in "'?' 192.168.8.1 192.168.8.0/24 $_TS_ROUTE" "phaedra '' '' $_TS_ROUTE" "phaedra 192.168.8.1 192.168.8.0/24 ''"; do
                 eval "set -- $_lf"
                 case "$(lan_fib_verdict "$1" "${2:-}" "${3:-}" "${4:-}")" in
                   n/a*) ;; *) _lfail "missing evidence [$_lf] did not render n/a" ;;
                 esac
               done
               # (L5) the probe target must be a LAN address that is NOT ours — `ip rule` 0
               # (`from all lookup local`) precedes the tailscale rule, so our own address answers
               # `local` no matter how thoroughly the LAN is swallowed, and would green the check
               # exactly during the fault.
               [ "$(lan_probe_target 192.168.8.224/24)" = "192.168.8.1 192.168.8.0/24" ] \
                 || _lfail "probe target for 192.168.8.224/24 was '$(lan_probe_target 192.168.8.224/24)'"
               [ "$(lan_probe_target 192.168.8.1/24)" = "192.168.8.2 192.168.8.0/24" ] \
                 || _lfail "probe target collided with our own address: '$(lan_probe_target 192.168.8.1/24)'"
               [ -z "$(lan_probe_target 10.0.0.1/32)" ] \
                 || _lfail "a /32 has no third address to probe with, yet one was offered"
               [ -z "$(lan_probe_target '')" ] || _lfail "an empty CIDR yielded a probe target"
               # (L5b) the SUBJECT of the probe. lan_probe_target/lan_fib_verdict decide correctly
               # about whatever prefix they are handed; lan_own_cidr decides WHICH prefix that is,
               # and until 2026-08-22 it took `head -1` of the global v4 addresses — ifindex order,
               # i.e. PLUG order. Live on mesh-home the operator's Note 3 tether (enx…, ifindex 3,
               # 192.168.42.130/24) outranked the real LAN (wlx…, ifindex 5, 192.168.8.224/24), so
               # every arm above ran perfectly against a network nobody defends and the card read
               # `ok` while 192.168.8.0/24 — the prefix the exit node swallows — was never asked
               # about. A detector bound to the wrong subject is green by construction, and the
               # healer that reads `net:` from it would have installed the throw on the tether.
               # Driven on the REAL function through a PATH shim, with the tether FIRST so the arm
               # goes red the moment the picker goes back to ifindex order.
               _ocd="$(mktemp -d)"
               cat > "$_ocd/ip" <<'OCIP'
#!/bin/sh
case "$*" in
  *"route show default"*)
      [ -n "$OC_NODEFAULT" ] && exit 0
      echo "default via 192.168.8.1 dev ${OC_DEFDEV:-wlxbcec43434a22} proto dhcp src 192.168.8.224 metric 600"; exit 0;;
  *"addr show dev wlxbcec43434a22 scope global"*)
      echo "5: wlxbcec43434a22    inet 192.168.8.224/24 metric 600 brd 192.168.8.255 scope global dynamic wlxbcec43434a22"; exit 0;;
  *"addr show dev "*) exit 0;;
  *"addr show scope global"*)
      echo "3: enx0257356d3666    inet 192.168.42.130/24 scope global noprefixroute enx0257356d3666"
      echo "4: tailscale0    inet 100.81.222.19/32 scope global tailscale0"
      echo "5: wlxbcec43434a22    inet 192.168.8.224/24 metric 600 brd 192.168.8.255 scope global dynamic wlxbcec43434a22"
      exit 0;;
esac
exit 0
OCIP
               chmod +x "$_ocd/ip"
               _ocg="$(PATH="$_ocd:$PATH" lan_own_cidr)"
               [ "$_ocg" = 192.168.8.224/24 ] \
                 || _lfail "lan_own_cidr picked '$_ocg' with a USB tether plugged in — it must follow the DEFAULT ROUTE's device (192.168.8.224/24), not ifindex order"
               # and the two silences degrade to the old ifindex read rather than to blindness: an
               # empty cidr renders `n/a (no usable LAN prefix)`, which is honest but is still a
               # detector that cannot fire.
               _ocg="$(PATH="$_ocd:$PATH" OC_NODEFAULT=1 lan_own_cidr)"
               [ "$_ocg" = 192.168.42.130/24 ] \
                 || _lfail "with no default route lan_own_cidr must fall back to the first physical address, got '$_ocg'"
               _ocg="$(PATH="$_ocd:$PATH" OC_DEFDEV=tailscale0 lan_own_cidr)"
               [ "$_ocg" = 192.168.42.130/24 ] \
                 || _lfail "a default route riding an overlay is not a LAN link — lan_own_cidr must fall back, got '$_ocg'"
               rm -rf "$_ocd"
               # (L6) and it must be WIRED INTO THE CARD. One REAL refresh over a scratch HOME
               # whose shimmed `ip`/`tailscale` present the swallowed-LAN fixture, asserting the
               # RENDERED line AND that it reaches invariant-check (which is what mesh-card-watchdog
               # alerts on — an unfolded verdict would be a warning nobody is wired to hear).
               # Same shimming rationale as arm (d): an unshimmed refresh SSHes to phones.
               _ltd="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$_ltd/.local/bin" "$_ltd/.mesh"
               cat > "$_ltd/.local/bin/ip" <<'IPSH'
#!/bin/sh
case "$*" in
  *"addr show scope global"*) echo "3: wlan0    inet 192.168.8.224/24 brd 192.168.8.255 scope global wlan0"; exit 0;;
  *"link show type wireguard"*) exit 0;;
  *"route get 192.168.8.1"*) echo "192.168.8.1 dev tailscale0 table 52 src 100.81.222.19 uid 1000"; exit 0;;
  *"route get "*) echo "1.1.1.1 dev tailscale0 table 52 src 100.81.222.19 uid 1000"; exit 0;;
esac
exit 0
IPSH
               cat > "$_ltd/.local/bin/tailscale" <<'TSSH'
#!/bin/sh
case "$1" in
  status) echo '{"Self":{"HostName":"t"},"Peer":{"p":{"ID":"XID","HostName":"phaedra","TailscaleIPs":["100.1.1.1"],"Tags":[],"Online":true}}}';;
  debug)  echo '{"ExitNodeID":"XID","ExitNodeAllowLANAccess":true}';;
  ip)     echo "100.81.222.19";;
esac
exit 0
TSSH
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_ltd/.local/bin/curl"
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 1\n' > "$_ltd/.local/bin/systemctl"
               for _p in mesh-camera mesh-body-motion mesh-light mesh-link-flap mesh-volume; do
                   printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 1\n' > "$_ltd/.local/bin/$_p"
               done
               chmod +x "$_ltd/.local/bin"/*
               HOME="$_ltd" MESH_CARD="$_ltd/card" "$SELF" --refresh >/dev/null 2>&1
               _lrc=$?
               grep -q '^  exit-node-lan: ⚠ SWALLOWED' "$_ltd/card" \
                 || _lfail "a swallowed-LAN REFRESH rendered '$(sed -n 's/^  exit-node-lan: //p' "$_ltd/card")' — the probe is right but the card does not use it"
               grep -q '^  invariant-check: ⚠ SWALLOWED' "$_ltd/card" \
                 || _lfail "the swallowed LAN did not reach invariant-check, so the watchdog would never alert on it"
               [ "$_lrc" = 2 ] || _lfail "a swallowed-LAN refresh exited $_lrc — mesh-card-watchdog alerts on non-zero only"
               # ...and the OTHER leg must stay silent. The fixture has no WireGuard interface at
               # all, so `default-egress:` must read (clean). Keyed on the rollup instead of its
               # own leg it would read "⚠ via WireGuard" — a summary asserting a leg that never
               # fired, and a second, false accusation riding in on the true one.
               grep -q '^  default-egress: .*(clean)' "$_ltd/card" \
                 || _lfail "the LAN leg libelled a clean egress: '$(sed -n 's/^  default-egress: //p' "$_ltd/card")'"
               # (L7) the STANDALONE probe `mesh-exit-node-lan-heal` consumes, over the same
               # swallowed fixture. The card rendering the verdict is not the same claim as the
               # actuator being able to READ it: the healer needs `net` and `table` as FIELDS, and
               # if it had to regex them back out of the English sentence, rewording the sentence
               # would silently disarm the re-application. So assert the fields, the state word and
               # the rc — the three things the healer actually branches on.
               _lkv="$(HOME="$_ltd" MESH_CARD="$_ltd/card" "$SELF" --exit-node-lan 2>/dev/null)"; _lrc=$?
               [ "$_lrc" = 2 ] || _lfail "--exit-node-lan over the swallowed fixture exited $_lrc, must be 2"
               [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$_lkv" | sed -n 's/^state: //p')" = swallowed ] \
                 || _lfail "--exit-node-lan state was '$(printf '%s\n' "$_lkv" | sed -n 's/^state: //p')', must be swallowed"
               [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$_lkv" | sed -n 's/^net: //p')" = 192.168.8.0/24 ] \
                 || _lfail "--exit-node-lan lost the net field: '$_lkv'"
               [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$_lkv" | sed -n 's/^table: //p')" = 52 ] \
                 || _lfail "--exit-node-lan lost the table field — the healer would have nowhere to install the throw: '$_lkv'"
               rm -rf "$_ltd"
               # --- S5 identity coherence (VSM PII2) -------------------------------------------
               # Every arm drives the REAL identity_conflicts/identity_verdict against fixture
               # surfaces. Mutants seen RED before landing (run from a scratch copy, 2026-08-04):
               #   (i)   drop the `seen[k] != v` guard              → arm (1) flags a plain re-export
               #   (ii)  tolerate a leading '#' (both the awk pattern AND the prefix `sub()` — it
               #         takes BOTH: with only the pattern loosened, a comment yields the mangled
               #         key "# MESH_A_CMD", which collides with nothing and the arm passes for the
               #         wrong reason. The realistic slip is the pair) → arm (3) counts a
               #         commented-out archaeology line, which is what restore.env is full of
               #   (iii) `return 0` instead of `return 2` on a missing restore.env → arms (7) both
               #         red: a node that could not be asked would read COHERENT
               #   (iv)  delete the render line from refresh()      → arm (9) red (unwired check)
               _itd="$(mktemp -d)"; _ie="$_itd/restore.env"; _ic="$_itd/card"
               _ifail(){ echo "smoke-test: FAIL (identity/PII2: $1)"; failures=1; }
               _in(){ identity_conflicts "$_ie" "$_ic" | grep -c . ; }
               # (1) identical re-assignment is redundancy, not two conceptions
               printf 'MESH_A_CMD="claude"\nexport MESH_A_CMD="claude"\n' > "$_ie"
               [ "$(_in)" = 0 ] || _ifail "an identical re-assignment was counted as a rival conception"
               # (2) differing re-assignment IS the pathology — and must name both lines
               printf 'MESH_A_CMD="claude"\nMESH_B=1\nexport MESH_A_CMD="opencode"\n' > "$_ie"
               _iout="$(identity_conflicts "$_ie" "$_ic")"
               printf '%s' "$_iout" | grep -q 'redeclared MESH_A_CMD: L1=.*-> L3=' \
                 || _ifail "a redeclaration with a DIFFERENT value was missed or lost its line numbers: '$_iout'"
               # (3) a commented-out declaration is not a declaration (restore.env carries several
               # `# MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="..."` history lines — counting them would be pure noise)
               printf 'MESH_A_CMD="claude"\n# MESH_A_CMD="opencode"\n#export MESH_A_CMD="codex"\n' > "$_ie"
               [ "$(_in)" = 0 ] || _ifail "a commented-out declaration was counted as live"
               # (4) a '#' inside a quoted value must not truncate the value it is compared on
               printf 'MESH_A_CMD="run --tag #one"\nMESH_A_CMD="run --tag #two"\n' > "$_ie"
               printf '%s' "$(identity_conflicts "$_ie" "$_ic")" | grep -q '#two' \
                 || _ifail "a '#' inside a quoted value truncated it — two distinct values would read as one"
               # (5) planted AND retired at once
               printf 'MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="genome tg"\nMESH_RETIRED_CHANNELS="room tg"\n' > "$_ie"
               identity_conflicts "$_ie" "$_ic" | grep -q '^planted+retired tg:' \
                 || _ifail "a channel declared both live and decommissioned was not flagged"
               printf 'MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="genome tg"\nMESH_RETIRED_CHANNELS="room diary"\n' > "$_ie"
               identity_conflicts "$_ie" "$_ic" | grep -q '^planted+retired' \
                 && _ifail "phantom planted+retired on disjoint channel lists"
               # (6) card says minds, policy says none (the ds regression — measured, not papered over)
               printf 'MESH_MIND_CHANNELS="none"\n' > "$_ie"
               printf '  minds: claude opencode\n' > "$_ic"
               identity_conflicts "$_ie" "$_ic" | grep -q '^card-vs-policy:' \
                 || _ifail "card 'minds:' vs a policy-OFF allowlist was not flagged"
               printf '  minds: (none)\n' > "$_ic"
               identity_conflicts "$_ie" "$_ic" | grep -q '^card-vs-policy:' \
                 && _ifail "a blanked card 'minds:' wrongly read as a conflict"
               # (7) no restore.env = CANNOT ASK. Never a silent COHERENT (n/a must be a claim
               # about the node, not a blank that reads as health).
               rm -f "$_ie"
               identity_conflicts "$_ie" "$_ic"; [ $? = 2 ] \
                 || _ifail "a missing restore.env did not return the cannot-ask code"
               case "$(identity_verdict "$_ie" "$_ic")" in
                 n/a*) ;; *) _ifail "a missing restore.env rendered '$(identity_verdict "$_ie" "$_ic")' — must be n/a" ;;
               esac
               # (8) a genuinely clean surface reads COHERENT (so the verdict is not stuck warning)
               printf 'MESH_A_CMD="claude"\nMESH_MIND_CHANNELS="genome"\nMESH_RETIRED_CHANNELS="room"\n' > "$_ie"
               printf '  minds: claude\n' > "$_ic"
               [ "$(identity_verdict "$_ie" "$_ic")" = COHERENT ] \
                 || _ifail "a clean fixture did not read COHERENT: '$(identity_verdict "$_ie" "$_ic")'"
               rm -rf "$_itd"
               # (9) and it must be WIRED INTO THE CARD — (1)-(8) only prove the functions decide
               # right. One REAL refresh through the real writer, over a scratch HOME whose
               # restore.env carries a known conflict, asserting the RENDERED line. Same shimming
               # rationale as arm (d) above: an unshimmed refresh SSHes to phones and blows the
               # timeout 30 that doctor/autowire impose.
               _istd="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$_istd/.local/bin" "$_istd/.mesh"
               printf 'MESH_A_CMD="claude"\nMESH_A_CMD="opencode"\n' > "$_istd/.mesh/restore.env"
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_istd/.local/bin/curl"
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_istd/.local/bin/tailscale"
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 1\n' > "$_istd/.local/bin/systemctl"
               for _p in mesh-camera mesh-body-motion mesh-light mesh-link-flap mesh-volume; do
                   printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 1\n' > "$_istd/.local/bin/$_p"
               done
               chmod +x "$_istd/.local/bin"/*
               HOME="$_istd" MESH_CARD="$_istd/card" "$SELF" --refresh >/dev/null 2>&1
               _got="$(sed -n 's/^  identity-coherence: //p' "$_istd/card")"
               case "$_got" in
                 *CONFLICTED*) ;;
                 "") _ifail "a real refresh rendered NO identity-coherence line — the check is not wired into the card" ;;
                 *)  _ifail "a real refresh over a conflicted restore.env rendered '$_got'" ;;
               esac
               rm -rf "$_istd"
               # --- DECLARATION vs ATTAINMENT + STICKY ROSTER (organ-roster-shrink-hides-a-dead-organ) --
               # Every arm drives a REAL --refresh through the real writer and reads the CARD, never the
               # detector's innards: the bug was that a working detector was wired to the wrong question.
               # Seen RED against HEAD before landing (all four arms): a card refresh with the body-probe
               # failing rendered `senses: ble irq link-flap` — body-motion simply GONE, live/total 8/8.
               # Mutants that must re-red it: (i) restore `mesh-body-motion --test &&` as the gate → arm
               # (r1); (ii) drop roster_declared from the render → arms (r2)+(r3); (iii) make _organ_retired
               # always false → arm (r4) (an unretirable roster is a leak, not a floor).
               _rtd="$(mktemp -d)"; _rbin="$_rtd/.local/bin"; mkdir -p "$_rbin" "$_rtd/.mesh"
               printf 'export PHONE_IP=203.0.113.99\n' > "$_rtd/.mesh/nodes"   # TEST-NET-3 black hole
               # every organ probe FAILS — the phone-gone state the card used to render as absence
               for _p in mesh-body-motion mesh-light mesh-camera mesh-volume mesh-link-flap arecord pw-record; do
                   printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 1\n' > "$_rbin/$_p"
               done
               printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_rbin/curl"; printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > "$_rbin/tailscale"
               chmod +x "$_rbin"/*
               _rcard(){ HOME="$_rtd" MESH_CARD="$_rtd/card" "$SELF" --refresh >/dev/null 2>&1; }
               _rsenses(){ sed -n 's/^  senses: //p' "$_rtd/card"; }
               # (r1) DECLARATION follows CONFIGURATION: the node has a body, so it declares the body
               #      sense even though the organ's own probe fails. THE find.
               _rcard
               case " $(_rsenses) " in
                 *" body-motion "*) ;;
                 *) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (body-motion vanished from the card when its probe failed — declaration is still gated on attainment) — [$(_rsenses)]"; failures=1 ;;
               esac
               # (r2) …and light degrades to its webcam/beacon tier, so a dead phone must not erase it either
               case " $(_rsenses) " in
                 *" light "*) ;;
                 *) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (light left the card with the body down — its webcam/beacon tier makes it a configured sense) — [$(_rsenses)]"; failures=1 ;;
               esac
               # (r3) STICKY FLOOR: erase the body from the config entirely. TOTAL must not shrink —
               #      only an explicit retire may remove an organ.
               : > "$_rtd/.mesh/nodes"; _rcard
               case " $(_rsenses) " in
                 *" body-motion "*) ;;
                 *) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (organ silently left the roster once detection went false — the sticky floor does not hold) — [$(_rsenses)]"; failures=1 ;;
               esac
               # (r4) monotone EXCEPT by intent: --retire-organ is the one exit, and it works.
               HOME="$_rtd" MESH_CARD="$_rtd/card" "$SELF" --retire-organ body-motion >/dev/null 2>&1
               _rcard
               case " $(_rsenses) " in
                 *" body-motion "*) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--retire-organ did not remove the organ — a roster with no exit is a leak, not a floor) — [$(_rsenses)]"; failures=1 ;;
               esac
               rm -rf "$_rtd"
               ((failures)) && exit 1
               echo "smoke-test: ok (+ services-active: bus-less → '?', bus-says-zero → 'none', mesh unit survives the filter, + a real bus-less refresh renders '?' so the probe is provably wired into the card; + identity/PII2: identical re-export ≠ conflict, differing one IS with both line numbers, commented-out ignored, quoted '#' kept, planted+retired, card-vs-policy, missing restore.env → n/a not COHERENT, and a real refresh renders the line; + organ roster: body sense declared on CONFIGURATION through a dead probe, light survives a dead body, sticky floor holds TOTAL when detection goes false, --retire-organ is the one exit; + exit-node-lan: the FIB and never the pref — a LAN routed via tailscale0 is SWALLOWED with a pasteable net+table remedy, a LAN on its own link is not, no-exit-node/unaskable/no-prefix/empty-answer all render n/a, the probe target is never our own address (rule 0 would answer it locally and green the live fault), the SUBJECT is the default route's LAN and not whatever was enumerated first (a USB tether outranked the real LAN by ifindex until 2026-08-22), and a real refresh renders the line, folds it into invariant-check so the watchdog alerts, and leaves default-egress clean; + --exit-node-lan: the standalone probe the re-application leg reads emits state/net/table as FIELDS with rc2 over the same swallowed fixture, so rewording the verdict cannot disarm the healer)"; exit 0 ;;
    "")        [ -r "$CARD" ] && cat "$CARD" || echo "no card at $CARD — run: mesh-card --refresh" ;;
    *)         echo "usage: mesh-card [--refresh]" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
